Why Ephemeral Storage Fails

Containers have an ephemeral root filesystem. If a pod running a database crashes or gets rescheduled to another node, all writes stored inside the container disappear.

To solve this, Kubernetes decouples storage into three layers:

StorageClass (SC): The storage backend blueprint (e.g., AWS EBS, GCP PD, or local hostpath in Minikube) that enables dynamic provisioning.

PersistentVolume (PV): An actual piece of allocated storage in the cluster.

PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC): A request/ticket submitted by an application declaring how much storage and what access mode it needs.


